//? Title: SOR: actual relaxed sweeps and recomputed residuals
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A sparse-structured linear system may require many Gauss-Seidel sweeps before
its residual is small. Successive over-relaxation changes each coordinate update
by a factor omega to accelerate suitable systems. We inspect actual iterates,
retain the original thirty-point1D Laplacian sweep, and report convergence flags
alongside iteration counts.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For the newest available coordinates, form the Gauss-Seidel candidate and blend
it with the old coordinate:

$$x_i^{new}=(1-\omega)x_i^{old}+\frac{\omega}{a_{ii}}\left(b_i-\sum_{j<i}a_{ij}x_j^{new}-\sum_{j>i}a_{ij}x_j^{old}\right).$$
\alttext(Each new SOR coordinate is a relaxed blend of its old value and the newest-value Gauss-Seidel candidate.)

Omega1 is Gauss-Seidel, below1 under-relaxes, and above1 over-relaxes. For
symmetric positive definite systems0<omega<2 guarantees convergence; the
interval alone does not guarantee convergence for arbitrary matrices. The
implementation checks shape, finite entries and nonzero diagonal, but does not
test positive definiteness. It recomputes the absolute Euclidean residual after
each complete sweep, including a real check at the initial zero iterate.

For the consistently ordered1D Dirichlet Laplacian with diagonal2 and
off-diagonal-1, Jacobi spectral radius is cos(pi/(n+1)), giving the asymptotic
spectral optimum

$$\omega_*=\frac{2}{1+\sin(\pi/(n+1))}.$$
\alttext(The standard1D Laplacian spectral optimum is two divided by one plus sine of pi over n plus one.)

A sampled omega with minimum finite-tolerance sweep count need not equal this
asymptotic value exactly. Dense implementation work is quadratic per sweep
despite the tridiagonal input pattern; reusable matrix-free or specialized
solvers are better for very large grids.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sor_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    a::SOROptions o;
    o.omega = 1.2;
    o.max_iterations = 3;
    o.tolerance = 1e-12;
    auto r = a::sor_trace({{2, -1}, {-1, 2}}, {1, 1}, o);
    std::print("| actual completed sweep | actual iterate | recomputed residual |\n| ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &s : r.sweeps)
        std::print("| {} | `{}` | {} |\n", s.iteration, s.x, s.residual);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Two-point Laplacian; omega1.2");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("{} actual coordinate updates",
                                     r.coordinate_updates));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Residual {:.4f}; converged {}",
                                     r.solution.residual, r.solution.converged));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/sor_tiny.svg");
}
```
