> API and interpretation

Empty or zero-RHS inputs can converge without a sweep; zero cap still reports
the actual initial residual. Finite tolerance is absolute and inclusive. Bounds
count actual coefficient visits, including separately recomputed residual
products. General non-SPD inputs may fail to converge or produce an explicit
arithmetic-range error. No elapsed-time speedup or universal best omega is
claimed.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Young (1954), Iterative methods for solving partial difference equations of elliptic type|https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1954-0059635-7]

